// SR 142.20 — Chapter 14c Data Protection under the Schengen Association Agreement
// generated by src/parse.py. Do not edit by hand.

:NOTE
Originally Chapter 14bis, then Chapter 14b. Inserted by Art. 127 below, in force since 12 Dec. 2008 (AS 2008 5405 Art. 2 let. a).
|

:NOTE
English is not an official language of the Swiss Confederation. This translation is provided for information purposes only and has no legal force.

:INFO
Art. 111a Disclosure of personal data
1 The disclosure of personal data to the competent authorities of states that are bound by one of the Schengen Association Agreements is regarded as equivalent to the disclosure of personal data between federal bodies.
2 SEM shall transmit personal data in accordance with Article 105 paragraph 2 to the European Union agency responsible for the surveillance of the Schengen external borders, provided these data are required to fulfil the tasks specified in Article 87 paragraph 1 letter b of Regulation (EU) 2019/1896. This form of disclosure is regarded as equivalent to the disclosure of personal data between federal bodies.
|

:NOTE
Art. 111a — Amended by Annex No 1 of the FD of 1 Oct. 2021 relating to the adoption of Regulation (EU) 2019/1896 on the European Border and Coast Guard, in force since 1 Sept. 2022 (AS 2022 462; BBl 2020 7105).
Para. 2 — See footnote to Art. 7 para. 1bis.
Para. 2 — Inserted by Annex No 1 of the FD of 1 Oct. 2021 relating to the adoption of Regulation (EU) 2019/1896 on the European Border and Coast Guard, in force since 1 Sept. 2022 (AS 2022 462; BBl 2020 7105).
|

:INFO
Art. 111b Data processing
1 SEM is the central authority for consultations in connection with visa applications under the Schengen Association Agreements.
2 In this capacity, it may use automated procedures to disclose and retrieve in particular the following categories of data:
a. the diplomatic or consular representation to which a visa application was submitted;
b. the identity of the person concerned (name, first names, date of birth, place of birth, nationality, place of residence, occupation and employer) as well as, if necessary, the identity of their next of kin;
c. information about the identity documents;
d. information about the places of stay and routes travelled.
3 The Swiss foreign representations may exchange data required at their location for consular cooperation with their partners from states that are bound by a Schengen Association Agreement, and in particular information about the use of forged or falsified documents and about human smuggling networks as well as data of the categories mentioned in paragraph 2.
4 The Federal Council may adapt the categories of personal data mentioned in paragraph 2 to the latest developments of the Schengen Acquis. For this purpose, it shall consult the Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner (FDPIC).
|

:NOTE
Para. 4 — Term in accordance with Annex 1 No II 4 of the Data Protection Act of 25 Sept. 2020, in force since 1 Sept. 2023 (AS 2022 491; BBl 2017 6941).
|

:INFO
Art. 111c Exchange of data
1 The border control authorities and the transport companies may exchange the personal data required in terms of the duty of care under Article 92 and the obligation to provide assistance under Article 93.
2 For this purpose, they may in particular disclose and retrieve the personal data in accordance with Article 111b paragraph 2 letters b–d.
3 Articles 109l, 111a and 111d apply mutatis mutandis.
|

:NOTE
Para. 3 — Amended by Annex 1 No 1 of the FD of 19 March 2021 on the Approval and Implementation of the Exchange of Notes between Switzerland and the EU on the Adoption of Regulations (EU) 2019/817 and (EU) 2019/818 on Establishing a Framework for Interoperability between EU Information Systems, in force since 15 June 2025 (AS 2025 347; BBl 2020 7983).
|

:INFO
Art. 111d Disclosure of data to third countries
1 Personal data may only be disclosed to third countries if they guarantee an adequate level of data protection in accordance with Article 16 paragraph 1 FADP.
2 If a third country fails to guarantee an adequate level of data protection, personal data may be disclosed to this country in the following cases:
a. the data subject has given their consent in accordance with Article 6 paragraph 6 and if applicable paragraph 7 FADP;
b. disclosure is required to protect the life or physical integrity of the data subject and it is not possible to obtain consent within a reasonable time; or
c. disclosure is required to safeguard overriding public interests or to establish, exercise or enforce legal rights in a court or before another competent foreign authority.
3 In addition to the cases mentioned in paragraph 2, personal data may also be disclosed if in specific cases adequate guarantees ensure appropriate protection of the person concerned.
4 The Federal Council shall determine the extent of the guarantees required and the modalities for providing the guarantees.
